{{also|sekt}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|de|Sect||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed in the 17th century, and provided with an additional ''-t'', from {{der|de|fr|sec|(vin) sec}}, that is "dry wine", which is also the original sense in German. The sense “sparkling wine” is believed to originate from an anecdote in 19th-century [[Berlin]]. The actor [[w:Ludwig Devrient|Ludwig Devrient]] supposedly ordered a bottle of wine using the phrase ''Bring [er] mir Sekt, Schurke!'', based on the German translation of the line "Give me a cup of [[sack]], rogue!" from [[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare’s]] ''[[w:Henry IV, Part 1|Henry IV]]''. He was served sparkling wine, his usual order, and this sense was given to the word ''Sekt'' when the phrase and anecdote caught on.

